          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "Your doctor's diagnosis is the right place to start for what it means for you. Here is what the general reference in our library says about the condition.\n\nType 2 diabetes is a problem with how the body handles glucose (sugar) in the blood. The body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a hormone that moves glucose out of the blood, and the pancreas cannot keep up with the extra insulin output this requires. As a result, blood glucose stays above the normal range. It develops gradually, over years. (Source: \"Type 2 diabetes mellitus,\" General clinical reference, educational summary.)\n\nThe same reference says many people have no symptoms when it is diagnosed. Its text on how the condition is recognized was cut off in the material I can access, so I can't tell you more about that section.\n\nThe reference also notes that the stage before diabetes, called prediabetes, is when lifestyle changes have the biggest effect. That is general background, not advice about your situation.\n\nOur library does not cover treatment options, medications, or what your own results mean. For those questions, ask your doctor, for example: What were my test results? What does my blood glucose target look like? What should I monitor, and how often? What changes, if any, do you recommend, and what should prompt me to call you sooner?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
