          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often should it be checked?",
          "answer": "The main \"how often\" answer in our library is about HbA1c, a blood test that reflects average blood glucose over roughly the past three months. The glycemic targets and monitoring document (National diabetes association standards of care, educational summary) says HbA1c is checked at least twice a year for people who are meeting their goal on a stable treatment plan. It is checked every three months for people who are not at goal, or whose treatment has recently changed. The same document notes that retesting sooner than about three months adds little, because the result still mostly reflects the earlier period. It also says that continuous glucose monitoring and home finger-stick checks complement HbA1c rather than replace it.\n\nThe library does not say how often a particular person should be checked. That depends on your results, your treatment, and your goals, so ask your doctor: How often should my HbA1c be checked? Do I need any home monitoring, and if so, how often? What would make you want to see me sooner?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
